+## Memory pool tracing
+
+Set `BOLT_MEMORY_TRACE_FILE` on any Bolt binary to record memory-pool allocation
+events. The trace is off by default and records only when this variable is
+present. The output is a versioned binary stream with interned pools, raw
+call stacks, executable mappings, periodic checkpoints, capture statistics,
+and a clean-shutdown trailer.
+
+```bash
+BOLT_MEMORY_TRACE_FILE=/tmp/bolt-memory.bin \
+  ./_build/Release/bolt/dwio/parquet/tests/reader/bolt_dwio_parquet_table_scan_test \
+  --gtest_filter=ParquetTableScanTest.basic
+
+python3 scripts/bolt_memory_trace_viewer.py \
+  /tmp/bolt-memory.bin \
+  -o /tmp/bolt-memory.html
+```
+
+Useful optional controls:
+
+```bash
+BOLT_MEMORY_TRACE_POOL_REGEX='.*Aggregation.*'  # only matching pool names
+BOLT_MEMORY_TRACE_STACKS=0                      # disable stack capture
+BOLT_MEMORY_TRACE_STACK_MIN_BYTES=4096          # stacks only above threshold
+BOLT_MEMORY_TRACE_BUFFER_BYTES=1048576           # batched write buffer
+BOLT_MEMORY_TRACE_CHECKPOINT_EVENTS=65536        # checkpoint interval
+```
+
+Stacks are captured as raw program counters on the allocation thread. The
+viewer resolves them offline with `addr2line` using executable mappings stored
+in the trace. This keeps symbolization out of the allocation hot path. If the
+matching binaries are unavailable, frames fall back to `module+offset`.
+
+The generated HTML is self-contained and can be opened offline. It shows the
+full-period memory usage timeline, pool distribution, allocation call sites,
+allocation lifetimes, live allocations at process end, and unmatched frees. The
+allocation flame graph uses vendored `d3` and `d3-flame-graph` assets under
+`scripts/vendor/`; they are embedded into the output HTML so hover details,
+search highlighting, and click-to-zoom work without a network connection. Click
+the timeline to rebuild the flame graph for live allocations at that point, or
+drag a time range to rebuild it from allocations overlapping the selected window.
+
+See `scripts/bolt_memory_trace_format.md` for the binary protocol.
+
+### Model-oriented analysis
+
+Use `bolt_memory_analyze.py` when an automated agent needs conclusions and
+bounded evidence instead of the complete event stream:
+
+```bash
+python3 scripts/bolt_memory_analyze.py /tmp/bolt-memory.bin \
+  --format json \
+  --top 5 \
+  --max-frames 8
+```
+
+The compact JSON output has a stable `schema_version` and includes:
+
+- an overall severity and confidence;
+- peak-live and pre-peak growth attribution by pool and stack;
+- post-peak release timing;
+- lifetime percentiles and suspicious long-lived allocations;
+- live-at-end allocations, unmatched lifecycle events, allocation churn, and
+  trace integrity findings;
+- bounded evidence and recommended next actions for every finding.
+
+The default exit code is zero after a successful analysis, regardless of
+findings. CI can opt into policy enforcement:
+
+```bash
+python3 scripts/bolt_memory_analyze.py /tmp/bolt-memory.bin \
+  --fail-on warning
+```
+
+Thresholds are explicit CLI flags. Common tuning options are
+`--long-lived-ms`, `--long-lived-ratio`, `--long-lived-min-bytes`,
+`--short-lived-ms`, `--short-lived-byte-ratio`, `--churn-ratio`, and
+`--peak-concentration`. Use `--format pretty-json` for manual inspection and
+`--format text` for a short terminal summary.
+
